  "query_preview": "(Saturday night through Wednesday)...\nA closed low pressure system moving southward off the California\ncoast will interact with a weakening tropical cyclone Rachel to \nspread moisture and precipitation into northern Baja and portions \nof southern California for Saturday night into Sunday.\n\nNBM probabilities for 0.10 inch or more of rainfall for Saturday\nnight through Sunday night range from 65 to 75 percent near the\ncoast to 80 to 90 percent in the mountains with 50 to 70 percent\nfor the deserts. NBM probabilities for 0.50 inch or more of \nrainfall for Saturday night through Sunday night range from 40 to\n50 percent near the coast to 65 to 75 percent in the mountains \nwith 30 to 40 percent for the deserts. NBM probabilities for 1 \ninch or more of rainfall for Saturday night through Sunday night \nrange from 20 to 30 percent near the coast to 45 to 55 percent in\nthe mountains with 15 to 25 percent for the deserts. NBM \nprobabilities for 2 inches or more of rainfall for Saturday night\nthrough Sunday night are 15 to 25 percent for the mountains.\n\nA second low pressure system moving southward along the California\ncoast could bring additional showers for Monday and Tuesday. For \nMonday through Tuesday night, NBM probabilities for another \n0.10 inch or more of rainfall range from 45 to 55 percent near \nthe coast to 55 to 65 percent for the mountains with 15 to 35 \npercent for the deserts. For Monday through Tuesday night, NBM \nprobabilities for another 0.50 inch or more of rainfall range \nfrom 20 to 30 percent near the coast to 35 to 45 percent for the \nmountains with less than 15 percent for the deserts. For Monday \nthrough Tuesday night, NBM probabilities for another 1 inch or \nmore of rainfall range from around 10 percent near the coast to \n15 to 25 percent for the mountains. \n\nNHC has Rachel falling below tropical storm strength before \nlandfall in northern Baja late Saturday night on a track that \nwould take it into northwest Arizona late Sunday evening. Slight \ndeviations from the NHC forecast are unlikely to cause any \nsignificant changes to the impacts to southwestern California for \nSaturday night through Sunday night.\n\nWinds across the coastal waters are expected to shift to the west \nby Sunday afternoon with gusts to 20 to 30 mph. The tide gauge in \nthe San Diego Bay shows water levels around 18 inches higher than\nthose of a year ago, that higher current sea level the result of\nthe thermal expansion of warmer water from a marine heat wave and\nthe strengthening El Nino and the associated most recent Kelvin\nwave moving northward along the California coast. Those higher\nwater levels combined with south to southwest swell and resulting\nsurf from Rachel and surge from the winds across the coastal\nwaters will increase the potential for coastal flooding, \nespecially on Sunday.\n\nDrying and warming is expected for next Wednesday with northwest \nflow aloft and weakening onshore lower-level flow.",
